    statement: "Keeps data and code protected while in use inside a hardware isolated environment that can prove what it is running, so the host operator and its administrators are outside the trust boundary.",
    origin: "Hardware enclaves arrived with Intel SGX in 2015 and the Confidential Computing Consortium formed under the Linux Foundation in 2019 to define the field; its 2022 definition made remote attestation an explicit requirement. Virtual machine scale isolation followed with AMD SEV-SNP, Intel TDX and Arm CCA, which cloud providers now offer as confidential virtual machines. Signal used SGX for private contact discovery from 2017, and Apple described attested servers for its Private Cloud Compute in 2024.",
    structure: "A trusted execution environment is created by the processor: memory assigned to it is encrypted with keys the host cannot read, and the hardware refuses access from the hypervisor, the operating system outside the environment, and anyone with physical access to memory. At launch the hardware measures the code and configuration loaded into the environment and signs those measurements with a key rooted in the processor vendor. A remote verifier obtains that signed evidence (the attestation report), checks the signature chain to the vendor, compares the measurements to the expected values, checks firmware and microcode versions against known good, and only then releases secrets (keys, data) to the environment, typically over a channel bound to the attestation. The workload inside processes the data and returns results. Verification policy is held by the data owner, not the host.",
