    systemsLens: "The stock is ambient authority: what code can do without being handed anything. In most systems it equals the process's full permission set. Capabilities drain that stock to zero and replace it with an explicit flow of references whose graph can be drawn. The reinforcing loop is in design: once components receive only what they need, interfaces get narrower, and narrower interfaces make leaks easier to see.",
    underStress: "It gets stronger as code quality falls, because a vulnerable component with few capabilities can do little even when fully controlled. It is weaker when references leak, since a leaked reference is authority in the hands of whoever holds it; strength depends on short lived or revocable references for anything that crosses a trust boundary.",
    adoption: "Start where the platform already offers it: file descriptors and handles, signed scoped URLs, WebAssembly sandboxes, per plugin tokens. Pick one plugin or extension system and change it from inherited process authority to passed capabilities. Remove globals that provide ambient authority in that subsystem. Add attenuation helpers so narrowing is one call. Make revocation real with proxies for anything handed outside the process.",
