    origin: "Fault isolation by bulkhead is older than computing; cloud providers formalised it as a reliability practice, and Amazon published Reducing the Scope of Impact with Cell-Based Architecture in 2023 alongside the Well-Architected guidance to use bulkheads to limit scope of impact. Multi account strategies, shuffle sharding and per region isolation apply the same logic to security: a compromise or a bad change should reach a bounded fraction. The July 2024 incident in which a single content update disabled roughly 8.5 million Windows devices at once, by Microsoft's count, showed what an unbounded radius looks like.",
    structure: "The organisation chooses units of isolation: cloud accounts or projects, cells, regions, tenants, clusters. For each class of asset (credential, host, deployment pipeline, administrator) it states the maximum fraction of customers, data or revenue that the asset may reach; that is the budget. Architecture then partitions so the budget holds: separate accounts per environment and per major system, cells that each serve a fixed share of customers with their own data and dependencies, a thin router as the only component that knows all cells, and credentials scoped to one partition. Changes roll out cell by cell with a bake time and automatic halt on error. Cross partition access goes through explicit, logged paths with their own credentials. The budget is tested by asking of any component: if this is fully compromised or fails, what fraction is affected, and the answer must be at or below the budget.",
