    id: "mm-trust-mutual-tls-mesh",
    name: "Mutual TLS mesh",
    family: "trust",
    tier: "proven",
    statement: "Authenticates and encrypts every service to service connection in both directions so the network carries no implicit trust and every call has a verified caller.",
    origin: "Google's Application Layer Transport Security (described publicly in 2017) authenticated every internal RPC with per job certificates years before service meshes made the pattern available outside. Istio, Linkerd and similar meshes attach a sidecar or node proxy to each workload, issue short lived certificates carrying SPIFFE identities derived from service accounts, and can require mutual TLS for all traffic. Kubernetes adoption made the mesh form common because workloads and addresses change too fast for host based trust.",
    structure: "A mesh certificate authority (or an external one it chains to) issues each workload a short lived X.509 certificate whose subject alternative name is the workload's identity, for example spiffe://cluster.local/ns/payments/sa/ledger. A proxy next to each workload intercepts outbound and inbound connections. On connect, both proxies present certificates and verify the peer's chain to the mesh root, so the server knows the client identity and the client knows it reached the intended service. Traffic between proxies is encrypted; the application sees plaintext locally and does not handle keys. Authorization policy at the receiving proxy states which caller identities may reach which service and method. A strict mode rejects plaintext, with a permissive mode available for migration. Certificates rotate automatically before expiry. The control plane distributes roots, policy and configuration, and its own access is the most protected part of the system.",
